    "updated_at": "2024-12-19T14:54:19Z",
    "body": "fwiw, i think this idea makes a lot of sense @alice. as you said, depending on how feasible it is\r\n>move other top layer nodes inside the dialog in the accessibility tree to ensure that they can be accessed.\r\n\r\nand i think you're saying it would only be the top layer nodes that would appear \"above\" the modal dialog in the top layer stack - so if there was a manual popover that existed on the page prior to the modal dialog being invoked - _that_ would become inert.\r\n\r\nbut from my experience working with product teams and their users, rarely has it worked out where if a user invokes a modal dialog, that they would be able to access or think to access content outside of the modal.  in the cases where content was actually accessed outside of the modal, it was often thought that the modal just wasn't implemented properly.... it's supposed to be modal after all.  So, moving any content which should be accessible into the dialog's a11y tree would both help with discoverability, and make it less likely people might think \"this is just another poorly implemented modal dialog\"",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
