    "body": "> Another approach to this problem, though probably not a workable one, might be to prevent elements outside the “blocking” top layer element from forming another non-blocking top layer on top of it. i.e., if your popover element is outside the dialog, it simply doesn’t get to be rendered on top of it.\r\n\r\nI'm afraid there will be use cases, though. E.g. a tooltip or select picker that is rendered \"old school\" as a popover at the end of the `<body>`. Yes, that can be done by moving the popover up into the DOM of the `<dialog>` but perhaps in some cases that can't be done. Or doesn't make sense - like if there's a single component that provides the \"one tooltip\" that can be attached to multiple things on the page. It might not want to be moved around in the DOM just to make it work.\r\n\r\n> I think if I could wave a magic wand, I would make the dialog \"blocking\" behaviour instead a function of the top layer under certain circumstances. Then, everything above the \"blocking\" top layer would be excluded from the inerting behaviour, and everything below it would be included.\r\n\r\nI liked this phrasing, since that's roughly what I was going for. A modal dialog creates a new \"block\" in the top layer stack. Everything below that point in the top layer stack is `inert`. Everything after it escapes inertness. So a new, nested modal dialog creates a fresh \"block\" and inerts the prior top layer elements.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
